# Person parameters ############################################################

Load functions_person_parameters.R

For ML person parameter estimation in the unidimensional model or projected model
(local item dependencies are neglected) perform

ML_ability_uirt(yj,        # Score-vector of person j
                param_uirt) # data.frame of alpha, delta, nu for all items

#output:
# root   = estimation of person parameter theta
# f2     = value of second derivative in estimation of theta

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

For ML person parameter estimation in the multidimensional model perform

ML_multi(alpha,   # matrix of discrimination parameters for MIRT (I x K)
         delta,   # vector of item intercepts (length = I)
         nu,   # vector of dispersion parameters (length = I)
         yj)  # response vector of one person (length = I)
         

output:
estimate = estimated person parameter, 
stdEr = stdEr from maxLik function
hessian = Hessian matrix

# Standard errors person parameters #############################################

For standard errors in the unidimensional model:

SE_uni(param_uirt, # c(alpha_tilde, delta_tilde, nu)
      dat,         # matrix item response row=person, column = item
      theta_hat,   # theta estimation
      theta_hatL2) # 

output: dataframe with
SE_Info = standard error based on Fisher information

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 

For standard errors in the multidimensional model:
SE_multi(alpha, # matrix of discrimination parameters in MIRT (IxK)
         delta, # vector of item intercepts in MIRT (length I)
         nu,    # vector of dispersion parameters in MIRT (length I)
        dat,   # matrix of item responses row=person, column = item
        theta_hat, # matrix of estimations for theta
        hessianm){ # list of hessian matrices

output:
out$SE_fisher = standard errors based on Fisher information
out$SE_hesse  = standard errors based on Hesse matrix

################################################################################

Limitations of the implementation: 
We applied Gauss-Hermite quadrature which is only suitable for small K. 
As a consequence, person parameter estimation functions are only implemented for K < 5.

The implementation inherits limitations of the countirt package: 
The look up grid to obtain CMP quantities via interpolation ranges for
mu between 0.001 and 200, and for nu between 0.01 and 50. We implemented
check_mu to check whether mu lies within these borders for the mirt and projected model.